 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Inputs:** Medium properties (extinction coefficient $\kappa_t(\mathbf{x})$, emission coefficient $\kappa_e(\mathbf{x})$, phase function $p(\boldsymbol{\omega}_o, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i)$), light source definitions (position, intensity, direction), and camera ray parameters (origin $\mathbf{x}_0$, direction $\boldsymbol{\omega}$).
-*   **Outputs:** Radiance $L(\mathbf{x})$ along the ray.
+*   **Inputs:** Medium properties (extinction coefficient $\sigma_t(\mathbf{x})$, emission coefficient $\sigma_e(\mathbf{x})$, phase function $p(\boldsymbol{\omega}_o, \boldsymbol{\omega}_i)$), light source definitions (position, direction, intensity), and scene geometry (for shadowing).
+*   **Outputs:** Radiance $L(\mathbf{x})$ along the view ray.
 
 **Data Structures**
-*   **Voxel Grid:** For deterministic methods, the medium is often represented as a 3D voxel grid storing $\kappa_t$, $\kappa_e$, and potentially precomputed incident radiance $L_{ri}$ [S1].
-*   **Shadow/Illumination Volumes:** For analytic/deterministic methods, shadow volumes from light sources and illumination volumes are computed to define integration segments [S1].
+The method typically relies on a discretization of the medium. Common structures include:
